    "q-140307": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140307",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=7.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
